{# EVERY TABLE VIEW IN THIS THEME, AS `bb_theme:table`.

   WHY A STYLE TEMPLATE AND NOT A ROW ONE. `bb_theme:table` renders the header
   and the body together and keys every cell against its column, so a component
   instantiated once per RESULT ROW cannot emit a column set at all (spec F2).
   Core's `table` style already runs its template once per result set, which is
   exactly the level that binding needs — so this file is the whole binding and
   no bakers code sits at the style level to reach it.

   AND WHY THERE IS NO PHP LEFT AT ALL. The human's instruction of 2026-09-03:
   *"der hook muss weg. die componente sollte automatisch passen ohne das man
   was altern muss."* `TicketTableHooks` derived three things and nothing
   replaced it, generic or otherwise. Two of the three were never ticket-
   specific and are derived HERE, from `views_view_table`'s own variables:

     - `columns` from `header` — the key, `content` as the label, `url` when
       Views made the column sortable, `attributes['aria-sort']` for which way,
       and `options.info[key].align` for a numeric column. All of it is already
       in the variables, which is why no hook had to put it there.
     - the cells, unchanged, including the one em-dash rule below.

   The third — the row's status accent — is HALF derived here, and the split is
   the point. A `state → running|attention|wait` map in a template that also
   renders `user_admin_people` would be the coupling the 2026-09-03 deletion
   removed: the state vocabulary is a ticket's, it is declared per state in
   `bb_core.workflows.yml`, and Twig cannot reach the workflow plugin manager
   at all. So the ROW ENTITY is asked what it IS — `getBbPhase`, one ask,
   guarded — and this file decides what that LOOKS LIKE.

   THE RAIL MAP LIVES HERE, and it was moved out of PHP deliberately. It sat
   first as a private `state → accent` table on the ticket and then for one
   day as `TypeWorkflows::accentForPhase()`; both returned a `bb_theme:table`
   vocabulary word — `running`, `attention` — out of the model layer, which is
   an entity making a rendering decision on the design system's behalf. The
   map belongs to the renderer:

     - a conductor on the row → the `running` (teal) rail.
     - workflow phase `review` → `attention` (amber), and review wins even
       when a conductor is also set: somebody has to look.
     - otherwise NOTHING. `wait` additionally tints the whole row and is
       unused here: an unassigned ticket is already named in the assignee
       cell. `idle` renders the same transparent rail an ABSENT accent
       renders, so emitting it would only restate the component's default.

   There is NO INTERFACE behind the ask: an entity with no workflow has no
   phase, and the `|default('')` below is what makes declining legal.

   THERE IS NO ROW TARGET, AND ITS DELETION IS THE SECOND HALF OF BB-413.
   This file used to ask `getBbRowUrl` as well and carry the answer as
   `data-row-url` on the `<tr>`. Measured before deleting it: no JavaScript and
   no CSS in this theme read that attribute, and its only readers were the tests
   asserting it plus two Playwright steps using `[data-row-url]` as a row
   COUNTER — which now count `.table__row`. The visible affordance of a row was
   always the identifier cell's link, rendered by that column's own formatter,
   and it is untouched. What went with it is the ONE argument that made a
   table-row interface look unavoidable: `TwigSandboxPolicy` allows only `id`,
   `label`, `bundle`, `get`, `toString`, `__toString` and the `get`/`has`/`is`
   prefixes, so `toUrl()` was unreachable from here — probed 2026-09-03,
   `{{ entity.toUrl().toString() }}` answers `Twig\Sandbox\SecurityError:
   Calling "toUrl" method on a "Drupal\bb_core\Entity\BbTicket" object is
   not allowed`. With no URL to fetch, that constraint no longer decides
   anything except the NAME of the one method still asked here.

   `|default('')` IS WHAT MAKES "ASK ANY ENTITY" LEGAL. A missing method throws
   under `strict_variables` and returns NULL without it; measured both ways on
   2026-09-03, `{{ e.getBbRowAccent|default('NONE') }}` answers `NONE` in both
   configurations while the bare access throws in one. (That was the method's
   old name; the guard is the same for `getBbPhase`.) So every ask is guarded,
   and the props are OMITTED rather than emptied when the answer is nothing —
   `accent` declares an enum, and `''` would fail the component's validator
   where an absent key falls back to the transparent rail.

   WHAT VIEWS OWNS AND WHAT THIS FILE OWNS. Views owns the column set, the
   per-field access check, the sort affordance and each column's FACE (through
   that field's formatter, which may build a cell array and render it through
   the `cell()` macro this component declares — so the faces stay one code path,
   spec F14). This file owns exactly one thing: putting each column's finished
   output into the `rendered` cell that column asked for.

   ONE EM-DASH RULE, NOT NINE. Spec F13 measured that
   `ViewsThemeHooks::preprocessViewsViewTable()` only appends to a column's
   `content` when `trim($field_output) != ''`, so a column with no value
   contributes NOTHING and absence is detectable here. That is what lets a
   single rule serve every empty column instead of seven `dash()` equivalents in
   seven formatters — and `team`, the one ticket column with no data path at all
   (spec F5: `bb_project` carries no `teams` field and BB-356 owns whether
   it should), reads the same faint em-dash as an unset conductor rather than a
   different kind of absence. The one column that is NAMED rather than dashed is
   the ticket assignee: `Unassigned` is a state somebody acts on, so its
   formatter renders for an empty item list and this rule never sees it.

   THE RULE IS `|striptags` AND NOT A BARE `|trim`, AND THAT IS A MEASUREMENT
   RATHER THAN CAUTION. F13's absence signal is only clean while `twig.config`
   `debug` is FALSE. With theme debug on — which is every DDEV and every
   developer's local — `FieldPluginBase::theme()` renders each field through
   `views-view-field.html.twig`, and the theme-debug comments make
   `trim($field_output) != ''` true for an EMPTY field, so Views appends content
   for every column and F13's signal is gone. Measured on this site: an empty
   `priority` arrives 247 bytes long and strips to 0, where `state` strips to 8.
   A bare `|trim` therefore renders a blank cell instead of the em-dash on every
   local, which is the same "empty cell that looks like missing data" the
   component's own macro documentation warns about. Stripping tags makes the
   test blind to the comments and identical in both configurations, and
   `TicketsViewTableTest` runs with debug ON so the naive rule cannot come back.

   AND IT KEEPS THE TAGS THAT ARE CONTENT WITHOUT BEING TEXT. Stripping ALL of
   them asks "is there text", which is a narrower question than "is there
   anything" — and the answer is wrong for a cell that IS a control. Measured on
   the case that motivated the rule, and that case is GONE: the retired
   `views.view.bb_group_members` had a `bb_group_member_remove` column whose
   whole output was a submit carrying an `aria-label` and a
   `data-bakers-member-uid` and no text node, so a bare `|striptags` emptied it
   and this rule drew the em-dash OVER the only affordance the row had. Four
   browser scenarios failed on it.

   THE RULE STAYS, AND ITS SUBJECT IS NOW EVERY OTHER TABLE VIEW. BB-435
   retired that view and its field plugin — the members surface is a form that
   renders its own items, so no control is smuggled through a Views field any
   more — and `GroupMembersRemoveCellTest`, which pinned the rule from that end,
   retired with the mechanism rather than being kept green against nothing. What
   did not change is why the rule is right: this template binds EVERY table view
   from one place, with no per-view seam, so the next field to output a control
   with no text node would hit exactly the same em-dash. Keeping a rule whose
   original subject is gone is the cheap half of that bet.

   THE ALLOWED LIST IS "CONTENT IN ITSELF", NOT "EVERY TAG", and the exclusions
   carry the meaning. `<a>`, `<button>`, `<input>`, `<select>` and `<textarea>`
   are things a person can act on; `<img>` and `<svg>` are things a person can
   see. A `<div>` or a `<span>` holding no text is not content — it is the empty
   cell this rule exists to catch — so admitting wrappers would put the blank
   cell back under a different name. PHP's `strip_tags()` drops HTML comments
   unconditionally, with an allowed list exactly as without one ("this is
   hardcoded and can not be changed with allowed_tags"); probed on PHP 8.4.20,
   a theme-debug comment block strips to whitespace under both forms, which is
   what keeps the two configurations identical.

   WHAT THIS RULE CANNOT SEE is a hidden input, because `strip_tags()` reads
   tag NAMES and not attributes. A form whose only elements are FormBuilder's
   own `form_id`, `form_token` and `form_build_id` therefore counts as content
   while showing nothing — so a field that has decided NOT to draw a control
   must not hand one over anyway. `GroupMemberRemove::render()` used to be where
   that was answered; BB-435 deleted it with its view, so the obligation now
   sits on whichever field next chooses to emit a form, and it is recorded here
   because nothing else states it.

   IT ITERATES `columns` AND NOT `row.columns`. The order is the TABLE'S and a
   row does not get to choose one, and reading the header's keys is also what
   guarantees a cell per column on every row: a column missing from a row's own
   map would otherwise arrive at the component as an absent cell, which renders
   an empty `<td>` and reads as data rather than as absence.

   `result` AND `rows` SHARE THEIR KEYS, which is what lets a row reach the
   entity that produced it. `ViewsThemeHooks::preprocessViewsViewTable()` moves
   the raw result set aside as `result` and rebuilds `rows` keyed by the same
   `$num` — *"Store rows so that they may be used by further preprocess
   functions"* — so `result[index]` is the `ResultRow` behind `rows[index]`.
   `_entity` is what both the SQL entity query and `SearchApiQuery::addResults()`
   write; `_object` is the datasource's typed-data adapter and is the fallback,
   because a Search API row whose original object could not be loaded carries
   neither. A row standing for no entity at all is legal and simply has no
   target and no accent.

   `{% set %}` IS THE CAPTURE, AND IT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN IDIOMATIC. Views
   hands each column a list of `{ separator, field_output }` render arrays; a
   `{% set %}` block yields a `\Twig\Markup`, which `TwigExtension::
   escapeFilter()` keeps intact (`TwigExtension.php:450`) so the cell prints it
   unescaped and exactly once. A plain string in the same field IS escaped and
   would print tag soup, and a render ARRAY is rejected outright by the
   component's validator — which is what forces the output to be rendered before
   it is mapped (spec F12, probe 1d).

   THE ONE SHAPE THIS FILE DOES NOT SERVE, stated rather than discovered. A
   table view whose every field label is empty gets `header = []` from Views
   ("Hide table header if all labels are empty"), and with no header there are
   no column keys, so such a table would render as rows of nothing. None of the
   five `style: table` views in this tree is like that, and the honest fix if
   one ever is would be to give it labels rather than to guess a column set
   here. The same applies to the `empty_table` option, which none of the five
   enables: its placeholder row is keyed `0` and has no result behind it.

   @see \Drupal\bb_core\Entity\BbTicket::getBbPhase()
   @see \Drupal\bb_core\Workflow\TypeWorkflows::phase()
   @see web/themes/custom/bb_theme/components/table/table.twig
#}
{# Occupancy teal, review amber. See the header. #}
{# What counts as content when there is no text. See the header. #}
{% set bb_content_tags = '<a><button><img><input><select><svg><textarea>' %}
{% set bb_columns = [] %}
{% for key, column_header in header %}
  {% set column = {
    'key': key,
    'label': column_header.content|default('') ~ '',
  } %}
  {%- if column_header.url|default('') -%}
    {%- set aria = column_header.attributes['aria-sort']|default('') ~ '' -%}
    {% set column = column|merge({
      'sort': aria == 'ascending' ? 'asc' : (aria == 'descending' ? 'desc' : 'none'),
      'url': column_header.url ~ '',
    }) %}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if options.info[key].align|default('') == 'views-align-right' -%}
    {% set column = column|merge({ 'num': true }) %}
  {%- endif -%}
  {% set bb_columns = bb_columns|merge([column]) %}
{% endfor %}

{% set component_rows = [] %}
{% for index, row in rows %}
  {% set cells = {} %}
  {% for column in bb_columns %}
    {%- set markup -%}
      {%- for content in row.columns[column.key].content|default([]) -%}{{ content.field_output }}{%- endfor -%}
    {%- endset -%}
    {% set cells = cells|merge({
      (column.key): markup|striptags(bb_content_tags)|trim is empty
        ? { 'kind': 'text', 'value': '—', 'emphasis': 'faint' }
        : { 'kind': 'rendered', 'markup': markup }
    }) %}
  {% endfor %}

  {% set source = result[index]|default(null) %}
  {% set subject = source._entity|default(source._object.value|default(null)) %}
  {% set component_row = { 'cells': cells } %}
  {%- if subject -%}
    {%- set phase = subject.getBbPhase|default('') ~ '' -%}
    {%- set row_accent = phase == 'review' ? 'attention' : (subject.hasBbConductor ? 'running' : '') -%}
    {% if row_accent %}{% set component_row = component_row|merge({ 'accent': row_accent }) %}{% endif %}
  {%- endif -%}
  {% set component_rows = component_rows|merge([component_row]) %}
{% endfor %}
{{ include('bb_theme:table', {
  columns: bb_columns,
  rows: component_rows,
}, with_context = false) }}
